= Comparative wiki research register =

'''Status: Active research register.''' Reviewed 2026-08-20. This page tracks external knowledge-system research relevant to BITwiki architecture, navigation, governance, coverage, community, accessibility, and security. A reviewed source is evidence, not automatic design authority.

== Why this exists ==

Wikipedia is not one system. It is simultaneously an encyclopedia, category graph, link graph, portal system, set of editorial processes, governance community, maintenance machine, multilingual federation, software ecosystem, and object of academic study.

The wider wiki/encyclopedia ecosystem is even more varied. BITwiki should learn comparatively rather than cargo-cult one English Wikipedia convention.

The research program is intentionally fractal:

<pre>
Wikipedia
  → English Wikipedia
  → Simple English Wikipedia / accessibility variants
  → language editions where governance or architecture differs materially
  → sister Wikimedia projects
  → Wikimedia governance / Meta-Wiki
  → wiki communities outside Wikimedia
  → online encyclopedias outside wiki culture
  → specialist knowledge systems
  → academic studies of all of the above
</pre>

BITwiki's priority is not translation-for-translation's-sake. The first comparison axis is '''simple ↔ technical localization''': how the same knowledge is progressively explained to newcomers, practitioners, and technical readers while preserving one coherent knowledge substrate.

== Research dimensions ==

Each knowledge system can be compared across:

* mission and scope;
* audience and reading level;
* simple-to-technical progression;
* editorial model;
* governance model;
* contributor identity and permissions;
* review / approval / deletion workflow;
* namespaces and content/admin separation;
* categories and taxonomy;
* portals / contents / outlines / lists / indices;
* semantic structure and metadata;
* quality assessment;
* automation / bots / AI;
* revision and provenance model;
* source/reliability policy;
* contentious-topic handling;
* abuse, censorship, legal, and contributor-safety exposure;
* language/localization model where materially relevant;
* scale, activity, and maintenance burden;
* software / extensions;
* licensing and reuse;
* distinctive strengths;
* known failure modes.

